  "interpretation": [
    "In every view most pixels counted as visibly wrong stop being wrong when ambient occlusion is removed from both renders.",
    "The ambient occlusion pass takes 8 samples per half-resolution texel with a per-texel random rotation from hash12(floor(gl_FragCoord.xy)), and nothing smooths it beyond a 2x2 joint bilateral upsample. The 1x frame has 0.25 occlusion texels per output pixel; the 4x reference averages 4 into each, sixteen times as many independent rotations, so most of the difference is estimator noise the 1x frame displays rather than geometric aliasing.",
    "The rotation is tied to the screen, not the surface, so under camera motion the noise pattern does not move with the scene. This is a candidate temporal shimmer source and is not yet measured under motion.",
    "water_reflection shows why the fraction and the mean must be read together: removing ambient occlusion more than halves its visibly wrong fraction but moves its mean absolute error by 2%. That is consistent with the noise landing on dim near-field pixels, where a small absolute error is a large relative one (occlusion runs only within 320 m), but where the noise lands was not measured."
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "One deterministic render pair per view and setting on one GPU; the fractions cover 921,600 pixels each but there is no across-device sample.",
    "Ablating a pass removes its signal as well as its noise, so the ambient-occlusion-off figure is not an achievable target, only an attribution."
  ]
